usb - what is plugged in

  usb                    the devices on the bus
  usb ports              every port, whether or not anything came of it
  usb controllers        how many host controllers are driven

Where a device is reads as controller-port.address: the port is
numbered the way it is labelled on the machine, and the address is
what the bus handed out when the device arrived.

Class is what a device says it is. A device answering `per interface`
puts the truth in its interfaces instead, and the bus copies that up so
this column always says something useful.

The driver column is the device manager's answer, not the bus service's
opinion: the bus reads the descriptors, hands the class, vendor and
product to the manager, and prints whichever manifest matched. `none`
means the machine sees the device and has nothing to run against it,
which a manifest in /lib/drivers and `driver rescan` will fix.

`usb ports` is the view for when a device is plugged in and does not
appear: a port that reads connected with no address against it is one
the machine sees and could not enumerate, which is a different fault
from a port with nothing in it. A port reading `companion` handed its
device to another controller, which is what happens to a full or low
speed device on a high speed bus.

Devices appear and disappear on their own. Nothing polls: a controller
interrupts when a port changes, and the bus walks only that controller.
